#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# klein_invariant_j

#% text
# `klein_invariant_j(tau)` gives Klein's absolute invariant `J(tau)`, for `im_part(tau) > 0`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `J(tau)` is invariant under the modular group: `J(tau + 1) = J(tau)` and `J(-1/tau) = J(tau)` (DLMF 23.15.7, normalized so that `J(im) = 1`). The j-invariant of number theory is `1728*J`.

#% item
# In terms of the modular lambda function, `J` is `4/27*(1 - lambda + lambda^2)^3/(lambda*(1 - lambda))^2`.

#% item
# Machine numbers and balls give numerical values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
klein_invariant_j(1.0*im)
#% output
#= 1.0 + 0.0im

#% cell
klein_invariant_j(0.5 + 0.8*im)
#% output
#= 0.008535608791971561 + 3.2682348319558855e-33im

#% cell
klein_invariant_j(2.0*im)
#% output
#= 166.375 + 0.0im

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Invariance under `tau -> -1/tau`:

#% cell
klein_invariant_j(0.3 + 1.1*im) - klein_invariant_j(-1/(0.3 + 1.1*im))
#% output
#= 2.220446049250313e-16 + 2.220446049250313e-16im

#% text
# At `exp(2*pi*im/3)` the value is 0:

#% cell
klein_invariant_j(exp(2*pi*im/3.0))
#% output
#= 2.322905700116782e-46 - 2.245265026760062e-46im

